### 7.6 Mermaid Syntax & Layout Best Practices (v1.6.0)

> **Adopted from** Boomi flow-map prompt §4 + MuleSoft API-Led HLD prompt §5 + retail HLD/diagrams worked examples (`examples/retail-personalized-offerings_*.md`). Phase 6 self-check enforces all 8 rules (`@02_Mandatory_Stop_Points.mdc`).

| # | Rule | Why | Apply To |
|---|------|-----|----------|
| **D1** | Use `<br/>` (with closing slash) for line breaks. **Never** `<br>` (renders as literal text in some Mermaid renderers). **Never** `\n` (renders as literal text everywhere). | Renderer compatibility | Every node label, every arrow label |
| **D2** | Prepend `%%{ init: { 'flowchart': { 'curve': 'linear' } } }%%` to every architecture diagram. For per-flow drill-downs, merge `'themeVariables': { 'fontSize': '16px' }` into the same init directive. | Straight lines = clearer architecture; larger font = readable drill-downs | Architecture (Phase 6 §5) and Data Flows (Phase 6 §7) |
| **D3** | **Layout order discipline:** declare nodes in this order — (1) source/channel nodes → (2) subgraphs with components → (3) target/SoR nodes → (4) connections → (5) styles & class assignments. | Mermaid layout: declaration order = vertical placement; connection order = arrow routing. Disciplined order = deterministic layout. | Every architecture diagram |
| **D4** | **Edge semantics:** solid `-->` = action / data flow / sync call · dashed `-.->` = governance overlay / grounding feed / response-return. Do not mix semantics on the same edge style. | Visual distinction between primary flow and supporting concerns | Every diagram |
| **D5** | **Multi-target arrows:** when a Process/Experience API fans out to multiple System APIs, use **descriptive action labels** (`"File Retrieval"`, `"NAV Delivery"`, `"Decrement Inventory"`) on the P→S arrows — not duplicated flow IDs (which already appear on the inbound subscribe/inbound arrow). | Reduces visual clutter; each fan-out arrow visually distinct | Process API fan-out diagrams |
| **D6** | **Async fire-and-forget:** Solace/event-triggered flows that don't return a response have **no** return arrow. Show one-way chain: `Source → Bus → E_API → S_API → Target` (no arrow back to Source). | Reflects actual semantics; avoids implying request-response | Cat-D async patterns |
| **D7** | **Event-bus as explicit intermediary:** when Solace / Anypoint MQ / Kafka is the bus between Source and Experience API, draw it as an explicit node — never `Source → E_API` directly. Pattern: `Source -->\|"Publish"\| BUS -->\|"Subscribe<br/>flow IDs"\| E_API`. | Architectural truth: events flow through the bus | Event-driven diagrams |
| **D8** | **No dangling System API / target nodes:** every `s-*-api` (or component representing a backend interface) MUST have an outbound arrow to its target system with a protocol label. The only exception is the async fire-and-forget terminal (D6). | Diagrams without target-side arrows are incomplete | Every System API in every diagram |

### 7.7 Diagram Pattern Recipes

#### 7.7.1 Architecture-HLD Recipe (Phase 6 §5)

Reference: `examples/retail-personalized-offerings_HLD.md` §5.

- **Layout:** Left-to-Right, ≤12 primary nodes
- **Subgraphs as visual zones** with dashed borders (no fill): `style ZoneName fill:none,stroke:<class-stroke-color>,stroke-width:2px,stroke-dasharray:5 5` — one subgraph per architectural layer (e.g., `"User Engagement"` / `"Agentic Layer"` / `"API Gateway & Governance"` / `"Integration Layer (API-Led)"` / `"Backend Systems"`)
- **Node shapes:** `[(...)]` for data stores · `([...])` for personas/channels (rounded) · `{{...}}` for gateways/policies (hexagon) · `[...]` for services/components (rect)
- **Class assignment:** every node via `class A,B,C agentforce` syntax — full 18-class brand palette (§7.1) is canonical; a 4-class minimal subset (`sf` / `ms` / `backend` / `user`) is the **proven minimal recipe** when product-level distinction isn't needed (maps to `salesforceCore` / `mulesoft` / `systemsOfRecord` / `engagement` from §7.1)
- **Edges:** action `-->`, governance/grounding `-.->`
- **Mandatory follow-up:** "How this works for you" 5-bullet block immediately after the diagram (≤25 words/bullet, panel-friendly "you" voice)

#### 7.7.2 Per-Flow Drill-Down Recipe (Phase 6 §7 Data Flows)

Reference: `examples/retail-personalized-offerings_diagrams.md` (4 worked capability flows).

- **One diagram per use-case capability** (cap at ≤4 diagrams per HLD; defer rest to Supporting Doc)
- **Init directive:** merge `curve: linear` + `themeVariables.fontSize: 16px` into a single `%%{ init: ... }%%` block
- **Subgraphs by logical zone** (Data Sources / MuleSoft API-Led / Salesforce Platform / Backend Systems)
- **Numbered step labels on edges:** `"1. Asks for<br/>product advice"`, `"2. Triggers 'Check<br/>Inventory' Action"` — 1-7 step sequence per flow
- **Mandatory follow-up:** "Strategy Summary" 3-4 bullet block after each diagram (architectural rationale, not narrative)

#### 7.7.3 Async / Event-Bus Recipe

- Apply rules **D6** + **D7** from §7.6
- Show Solace / Anypoint MQ / Kafka as **explicit intermediary** node — class `mulesoft` if Anypoint MQ, else `customerInfra` if customer-owned Solace/Kafka
- **No return arrow** for fire-and-forget patterns
- For **pub/sub bidirectional** patterns, produce **two** diagrams: Publishers→Bus and Bus→Listeners (per MuleSoft API-Led HLD prompt §5.6)

#### 7.7.4 Round-Trip Return-Path Recipe

- Forward arrows: solid `-->`
- Response/data-return arrows: dashed `-.->`
- Group request → target → response-back arrows together before the next call chain (Mermaid layout hint)
- Use **short labels** (`"File Data"`, `"Response"`) — not flow IDs

